<Hub pillarId="crypto-basics">
  <p>
    Crypto is not one product. <a href="https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/spotlight/crypto-assets">Investor.gov</a> uses “crypto asset” for anything generated or transferred on a blockchain or a similar ledger, including coins, tokens, and virtual currencies. Two assets with different designs can carry different risks.
  </p>
  <p>
    Bitcoin is the one most beginners meet first. <a href="https://bitcoin.org/en/faq">Bitcoin.org</a> describes it as money controlled by cryptography rather than a central office, with the first public specification published in 2009. One bitcoin splits into 100,000,000 satoshis, so a first look does not require a whole coin.
  </p>
  <p>
    A wallet does not hold a picture of a coin. The <a href="https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins/crypto-asset-custody-basics-retail-investors-investor-bulletin-0">SEC's custody bulletin</a> says it stores private keys, the secrets that can spend the asset. Lose the secret and the access is gone. There is no reset letter from bitcoin itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    Labels from US authorities are not safety scores. The <a href="https://www.cftc.gov/LearnAndProtect/AdvisoriesAndArticles/understand_risks_of_virtual_currency.html">CFTC</a> describes bitcoin as a commodity. The <a href="https://www.irs.gov/filing/digital-assets">IRS</a> treats digital assets as property. The <a href="https://www.fdic.gov/resources/deposit-insurance/financial-products-not-insured">FDIC</a> lists crypto assets among products that are not deposit-insured.
  </p>
  <p>The guide below stays with bitcoin, in plain language, and links every factual claim.</p>
</Hub>
